{"Guest emails are compared against the lowercased blacklist without lowercasing the guest email first, so submitting Blocked@Email.COM bypasses the blacklist entry blocked@email.com": 0.9791702032089233, "addGuestsHandler requires a user to be BOTH team admin and team owner to pass the team permission check, so accepted ADMIN members (non-OWNER) cannot add guests unless they are also the booking organizer or an attendee": 0.9431551694869995, "The original unfiltered guests input is passed to sendAddGuestsEmails instead of the filtered uniqueGuests, causing existing attendees whose emails were re-submitted to incorrectly receive AttendeeScheduledEmail instead of AttendeeAddGuestsEmail": 0.9351531267166138, "The backend schema for addGuests accepts duplicate emails in the guests array, and addGuestsHandler does not dedupe within the request, so a crafted request can create multiple attendee rows for the same email and cause repeated calendar/email updates": 0.9817371368408203, "sendAddGuestsEmails always sends organizer and attendee emails and does not honor disableStandardEmails metadata checks used by other booking email flows, so events configured to suppress standard emails will still send guests added notifications": 0.9571958780288696, "addGuestsHandler swallows sendAddGuestsEmails failures with a console.log that drops the underlying error, making production failures hard to detect and debug": 0.896289587020874, "The guests array in the input schema lacks a maximum length constraint, allowing an attacker to submit an arbitrarily large array of emails causing mass spam and resource exhaustion": 0.9167721271514893, "Adding guests does not check the event's seatsPerTimeSlot limit, allowing attendees to add guests and exceed the event's maximum capacity, bypassing overbooking constraints": 0.9643620252609253}